httpant is a C++23 module library, and it is sans-I/O by default: it parses and
serializes HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 and moves no bytes for you.

    find_package(httpant CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE httpant::httpant)

    import httpant;

The native I/O engine is the `io` feature. With it enabled, an extra target
compiles `import httpant.io;` with asio as the default on every platform:

    vcpkg install httpant[io]
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE httpant::httpant httpant::io)

    import httpant.io;

The package uses the backend selected when it was built: `httpant[io,uring]`
selects io_uring on Linux; otherwise `httpant[io]` selects asio.

`httpant::httpant` links `httpant::imports` (the module wrappers over llhttp,
nghttp2, nghttp3 and stdexec) publicly; consumers never import it directly.

The `tls` feature adds wolfSSL and the transport-generic TLS module:

    vcpkg install httpant[tls]
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE httpant::tls)

    import httpant.tls;

The caller supplies the byte stream. Select `httpant[io,tls]` to also use the
native I/O engine.

The compiler must build C++23 modules: `find_package(xclang CONFIG)` has to
succeed, and the standard library must be available as a module (`xclang::std`).
